    On 0003: a `pattern` in a `response_format` reaches _visit_pattern() as client data, and three
    of its paths do not survive that. `front()`/`back()` run on an empty string; `seq.back()` runs
    on an empty vector for a leading quantifier; and a stray ']' or '}' -- in NON_LITERAL_SET, but
    claimed by no arm of the scanner -- makes the literal arm break without consuming it, so the
    loop reconsiders the same byte forever. core/text/chat.cpp:148 catches what the converter
    throws, so its std::stoi range errors already come back as a clean refusal; a hang and a
    read off the end of a container are the two things a catch cannot turn into a 400. The patch
    adds a size test, two empty-container guards, and a did-we-consume-a-byte test at the bottom
    of the loop, each recording an error the existing check_errors() already turns into a throw.

    On 0004: three things upstream ACCEPTS and then gets wrong, each now refused by name.
    A remote $ref is fetched through a callback the public entry point stubs out, so it silently
    degrades into "accepts any object" -- we refuse and fetch nothing, because a sampler that
    reaches the network to decide what tokens are legal is not one we ship. `"not"` has no case in
    visit(), so it falls through to a rule accepting any value -- a constraint the caller asked
    for, did not get, and is not told about. And a regex escape GBNF cannot spell (\d \w \s \b
    \/ \-, a backreference) is copied into the output verbatim, so the converter returns success
    and the grammar then fails to LOAD.

    0003 and 0004 together are what core/sample/json_schema.cpp used to carry on its own. That was
    a SECOND, independently hardened lift of this same file -- and, checked against a running
    server, one that NO REQUEST COULD REACH: response_format on /v1/chat/completions is compiled
    by THIS copy through the chat template, and on /v1/completions it is refused by the endpoint
    accept-list before the fork's seam is called. So the hardened copy never ran and the copy that
    serves every structured-output request was the unhardened one. The fork is deleted and its
    refusals are here, which is where they were needed all along.

