REWRITTEN INTO core/ INSTEAD
----------------------------

spec §12 lists these as lifts. Each one turned out to be a file we would have had to modify
heavily, and a file you modify heavily is a file that belongs in core/ under our own name -- so
it is there, with a comment at the top naming what it was read from.

  src/llama-grammar.{h,cpp} -> core/sample/gbnf.{h,cpp}
      MIT, same upstream commit. The GBNF parser and the stack machine, element for element; it
      is under core/sample/ rather than here because it is on the SAMPLING path and the sampler
      owns it, where vendor/ belongs to the text frontend. gbnf.h's own header lists what changed
      (llama_vocab became VocabView, GGML_ASSERT became status returns, and the parser gained the
      partial-UTF-8 and lazy-trigger handling the server needs) so a re-lift knows what to
      re-apply. Recorded here because it was not, and an MIT lift belongs in this list whichever
      directory it sits in.

  ggml/src/gguf.cpp        -> core/text/gguf.{h,cpp}
      Upstream is a reader, a writer and a ggml_context builder welded together: it fills
      `struct ggml_tensor`, calls ggml_blck_size(), and includes ggml.h for both. Taking it whole
      means taking ggml, which §12 says we do not. The wire format is a header, a KV table, a
      tensor directory and a padded blob, so the reader is a page of code. The one thing kept
      verbatim is the block geometry of every ggml dtype, which is data rather than code and is
      marked as such at the table.

  src/llama-vocab.cpp      -> core/text/tokenizer.{h,cpp}
      Welded to llama_model_loader, GGML_ASSERT and the llama_vocab class, and organised around a
      pre-tokeniser identified by HASHING tokenizer.json against a hardcoded enum -- which is the
      behaviour spec §12 exists to replace. The BPE bigram queue and the UGM Viterbi are
      re-expressed with attribution; the enum is gone, and a declared RadVocabStep chain drives
      the tokeniser instead.

  convert_hf_to_gguf.py    -> core/text/tokenizer_json.cpp
      Python, so not liftable at all. parse_tokenizer_json() interprets the declared
      normalizer / pre_tokenizer / decoder chain and refuses, by name, any component it cannot
      express -- rather than approximating it.

  (no upstream)            -> core/text/unicode_norm.{h,cpp}, unicode_norm_data.cpp
      llama.cpp has no NFC/NFD/NFKC/NFKD. Its `unicode_ranges_nfd` is a strip-accents table for
      the WordPiece path, not a canonical decomposition. The tables here were generated once from
      a system ICU; unicode_norm_data.cpp documents the recipe. radiance links no ICU.


PATCH SERIES
------------

  patches/0001-unicode-reject-codepoints-above-U-10FFFF.patch

      unicode_cpt_from_utf8() accepts a 4-byte sequence with a 0xF5..0xF7 lead and returns a
      codepoint above U+10FFFF. unicode_cpt_to_utf8() then throws std::invalid_argument, and
      nothing on the tokenise path catches it -- so four arbitrary bytes in a prompt terminate
      the process. Measured: 93 of 15,000 random byte strings do it.

      The patch rejects the out-of-range result at the decode site, where the existing
      unicode_cpts_from_utf8() handler already turns an invalid_argument into U+FFFD. Every
      sequence upstream accepts still decodes to the same codepoint, so nothing that worked
      changes; only the crash does. Verified by re-running the 15,000-string comparison against
      llama.cpp: identical output on all 14,907 strings it survives.

  patches/0002-chat-unclosed-reasoning-must-not-swallow-a-tool-call.patch

      analyze_reasoning::build_parser() ends a reasoning block with `until(</think>)`, and
      `until` returns the REST OF THE INPUT when its delimiter never arrives. So a reply whose
      reasoning block the model never closed was captured whole as reasoning_content -- and when
      that reply contained a <tool_call>, the call went with it: parsed as reasoning text, not as
      a call. The turn then carried no tool call and no content, and an agent driving the server
      stopped dead. Templates shipped for this model family carry explicit self-heals for the
      same shape, which is how we know models emit it.

      The patch adds an ordered choice whose first branch is guarded by a peek: reasoning may
      also end at the tool-call opener, but only when the opener is what actually stopped it.
      Every reply that closes its reasoning block takes the second branch, which is upstream's
      rule unchanged -- including reasoning that merely MENTIONS an opener, since such a reply
      still has its closing tag and the peek never fires. The marker is handed down from
      autoparser::build_parser(), the one scope that can see both the reasoning and the tool
      analyzers. tests/chat_test.cpp pins all three shapes against the real Qwen3.8 template.

  patches/0005-chat-an-xml-argument-value-owns-its-indentation.patch

      common_chat_peg_mapper::map() strips "one leading whitespace character" from every tool
      argument value. For Qwen's XML the grammar has ALREADY consumed the newline after
      <parameter=x> -- the differential analyzer derives it as the value prefix -- so the
      character that gets stripped is the first SPACE of the value. An agent's edit tool matches
      old_string against the file byte for byte, so `    return a + b` arrives as
      `   return a + b` and the edit never applies, on a model that wrote a perfectly good call.
      Every indented hunk, every call, silently.

      The patch strips the DELIMITER instead: one leading newline (\n or \r\n) and one trailing
      one, and nothing else. Values with no surrounding newlines are unchanged, which is every
      JSON-format call and every python-style one; only the tagged XML path can differ, and there
      the delimiter is exactly what upstream meant to remove. tests/chat_test.cpp pins an indented
      value and a multi-line one -- blank line, tab, trailing spaces -- against the real template.

  patches/0006-jinja-min-and-max-over-a-sequence.patch

      The engine has `sort` and `reverse` for an array but neither `min` nor `max`, and the
      undefined-value table lists both -- so the omission is in the array table, not in the
      design. A template that uses one throws mid-render, and the CAPABILITY PROBE SWALLOWS THE
      EXCEPTION ("ignore exceptions during capability analysis"), so the template comes back
      reporting tools=0 tool_calls=0: indistinguishable from a model that genuinely cannot call
      tools. MiniCPM5-2B's tool block sets `[tool_calls_count, tool_sep_count]|min`, and the
      symptom was /v1/chat/completions refusing `tools` with a 400 naming the MODEL.

      jinja2 semantics: the smallest (largest) ITEM of the sequence, by `attribute` where one is
      given, undefined for an empty sequence, `case_sensitive` accepted and ignored -- which is
      what the `sort` beside it does with the same argument.

  patches/0007-autoparser-split-the-per-argument-suffix-from-the-call-close.patch

      analyze_arguments() takes everything after an argument's value as `value_suffix` and then
      trims `function.close`, `per_call_end` or `section_end` off it. A template that declares
      none of the three has nothing to cut against and keeps the lot. MiniCPM5-2B writes
      `<function name="F"><param name="A">V</param></function>`, so the suffix came out
      `</param></function><|im_end|>\n` with an EMPTY close, and no tool call could ever parse: a
      second argument because the suffix closes the whole call, and even the FIRST because a
      generated turn ends at `<|im_end|>` and never carries the newline the template writes
      BETWEEN turns. Every call came back to the client as prose.

      The boundary is already rendered, one phase later, by extract_argument_separator(): between
      the first argument's value and the second argument's name marker is exactly the
      per-argument suffix. The patch measures it there and splits the tail -- value_suffix
      `</param>`, close `</function><|im_end|>` -- and declines, leaving upstream's result, if the
      markers do not line up. Only reached when the derived close is empty, so every template
      that already worked is untouched.

      The close is measured off a TWO-CALL render, not off the tail of a one-call one: the tail is
      what a template writes after the LAST call of a turn, so it carries the turn terminator, and
      a close demanding `</function><|im_end|>` can only ever match a reply holding exactly one
      call. Between the end of one call and the opener of the next there is no terminator.
      core/server/adapters.h still strips a trailing terminator from content, for the paths where
      the parser does not consume one.

  patches/0008-autoparser-tagged-calls-separate-on-space-and-named-args-in-any-order.patch

      TWO strictnesses in build_tool_parser_tag_tagged(), both of which turn a good call into
      prose. The rule ends in p.end(), so neither failure is partial: an unmatched call fails the
      whole parse and the optional `tool_calls` then matches NONE of them.

      build_tool_parser_tag_tagged() separates parallel calls with a literal ", " -- the separator
      of a JSON ARRAY of calls -- for a format whose calls are TAGS. MiniCPM5-2B writes
      `<function name="ls">..</function>\n<function name="bash">..</function>`, so the second call
      was required to begin with a comma and a space and never did.

      The failure is not partial. The rule ends in p.end(), so a trailing unmatched call makes the
      whole parse fail, and `tool_calls` being optional then matches ZERO of them: every reply
      with two calls in it came back as raw prose with the reasoning and the tags still in it. One
      call parsed, two never. Found by reading an agent transcript, where the client had parsed
      the first call out of that prose itself and silently dropped the second.

      A tagged call opens with its own name prefix and closes with its own close, so nothing
      between them is needed to tell one from the next: the separator is p.space(), which accepts
      the newline these models write and the nothing a template writing them adjacently produces.

  patches/0009-chat-a-cdata-section-is-punctuation-not-content.patch

      MiniCPM5's template instructs the model, verbatim: "If it contains <, & or newline
      characters, wrap it in a CDATA block: <param name="p"><![CDATA[...]]></param>". The mapper
      does not know that, so a `write` call arrived with `<![CDATA[` and `]]>` inside the argument
      value and every source file an agent wrote would have carried them.

      THE TWO ENDS ARE STRIPPED INDEPENDENTLY, and that is the whole of it. A streamed value is
      re-parsed from the top on every chunk and the server sends only what is new, so a value
      already emitted cannot be taken back. Requiring both delimiters before removing either let
      the opener escape in the first chunk every time -- files came out beginning `<![CDATA[` and
      ending `]]`, the model read back its own corrupted output, and it spent the rest of the
      session trying to edit the markers out. Anything that could still BECOME the opener is held
      back whole while the value is partial, as is a trailing `]` or `]]`, which is either the
      start of the closer or the end of a JSON array; withholding keeps what is emitted a prefix
      of the finished value, which is what the streaming diff requires.

      Same family as 0005: the punctuation of an XML argument value is not part of it.

  On 0008, the second half: REQUIRED ARGUMENTS WERE REQUIRED IN SCHEMA ORDER.

      A tagged argument carries its own name -- `<param name="path">..</param>` -- so its
      position means nothing, and a model emits them in whatever order it composed them. For a
      write that is very often the content first, because that is the part it was thinking about.
      Found in an agent transcript: the file was written, the model reviewed its own code, and
      its follow-up `write` came back as prose ending
      `]]></param><param name="path">/work/tic_tac_toe.py</param></function>`.

      Every argument is accepted in any order now, required and optional alike. What the grammar
      gives up -- checking that each required argument appears exactly once -- was never its job
      here: a call missing one is a bad call, and arriving at the tool AS a bad call says so,
      where arriving as prose says nothing at all. Scoped to the tagged builder; the JSON and
      positional builders keep their ordering.

  On 0008, the third: NOTHING WAS ALLOWED BEFORE THE CALL.

      `content_before_tools` is what lets a model say a sentence and THEN call a tool, and it is
      driven by the section or per-call opener. A format with neither -- this one -- got p.eps(),
      which permits no content at all, so a reply opening "The workspace is empty. I need to
      create a Tic Tac Toe implementation." and then making a well-formed call did not match, and
      p.end() made that a whole-parse failure. What opens a call in a tagged format is the
      function name prefix, so that is the marker to read up to; it is a fallback only, since a
      format that really has a section or per-call opener starts earlier in the text than the
      function name does.

  On 0008, the fourth and fifth: CONTENT AFTER THE CALLS, AND BETWEEN THEM.

      The same trap with the ends swapped. The rule was reasoning + optional(content) + calls +
      p.end(), so a model that signs off after a call, or narrates between two of them ("Next, I
      will check the current directory."), failed the whole parse. Content is now allowed after
      the last call and before each subsequent one, read up to the same trigger marker.

      All five of 0008's halves are one bug wearing different hats: the tool-call rule ends in
      p.end(), so ANY mismatch anywhere fails everything and `tool_calls` being optional then
      matches NONE. There is no partial credit, so every strictness in this grammar -- a comma, a
      terminator, an argument order, a sentence -- presents identically, as a model that cannot
      call tools.

  On 0008, the sixth: A NON-STRING ARGUMENT THAT IS NOT VALID JSON.

      An array or object parameter is matched by a schema-constrained JSON parser, and models do
      not reliably write JSON inside an XML tag. MiniCPM5 writes Python:
      `<param name="edits">[{'oldText': 'a', 'newText': 'b'}]</param>`. The JSON parser cannot
      begin on a single quote, so the argument did not match, so the call did not, so the reply
      came back as prose -- and the model, reading that as a rejection, rewrote the same call
      four times, once reordering its arguments to appease it.

      Strict JSON is still tried FIRST, so nothing that parses today parses differently. The
      fallback takes the value the way a string argument takes it, as the text up to `</param>`,
      which is what actually delimits it in this format -- and the mapper already normalises a
      Python-style container to JSON from there (normalize_container_value, which upstream wrote
      and the grammar never let it reach). What is given up is schema VALIDATION of the argument.
      A mistyped argument reaching the tool as a mistyped argument is strictly better than a
      correct call reaching the user as prose.

0010-autoparser-an-undeclared-argument-must-not-lose-the-call.patch
      chat-auto-parser-generator.cpp. Every argument arm matches its parameter name as a LITERAL,
      so an argument the schema does not declare matches nothing, the call matches nothing, and
      the tool rule's `p.end()` makes that THE WHOLE REPLY -- which reaches the caller as prose
      with the XML in it. Observed on a live MiniCPM5-2B agent: a `read` tool whose only parameter
      is `path`, called as `<param name="action">read</param>`, and the turn was lost. The cost is
      not limited to the malformed argument: a call that is entirely valid is lost when one extra
      argument sits beside it, which is the case the second test covers.

      A generic arm is appended LAST to the ordered choice, matching any name up to the name's own
      delimiter and taking the value the way a string argument takes it. Declared names still bind
      to their own schema-checked arms first, so nothing that parsed before parses differently.
      What is given up is knowing the argument was undeclared -- the TOOL gets to say so, properly,
      and a reply that never arrives cannot.

      THE DELIMITER AFTER THE NAME IS NOT ALWAYS `name_suffix`: this format decomposes
      `<param name="path">v</param>` as prefix `<param name="`, name_suffix EMPTY and value_prefix
      `">`. The first cut guarded on `!name_suffix.empty()` and so never built its own arm, built
      clean and changed nothing. Read the analysis rather than assuming the decomposition.

  patches/0011-chat-add_bos-must-come-from-the-inputs.patch

      `common_chat_templates_inputs` carries add_bos and add_eos, and the jinja conversion
      overwrote both from the TEMPLATES object -- which is only ever filled inside
      common_chat_templates_init, off a llama_vocab. A host that passes `model = nullptr` and
      supplies the vocabulary's facts as overrides had both flags stuck at false whatever it
      asked for, and that is exactly the shape of this lift's use here: the .rad carries the
      template, the BOS/EOS spellings and the add flags, so there is no llama_model to read.

      The flag strips a BOS the TEMPLATE rendered, because the tokeniser is about to add one. Off,
      a container whose tokeniser adds BOS and whose template also writes `bos_token` gets two.
      The Llama-family templates write it and Qwen's do not, which is why nothing here saw it.

      The two upstream callers that do set the field -- common_chat_format_single and
      common_chat_format_example -- assign `inputs.add_bos = tmpls->add_bos` immediately before
      calling apply, which is the redundancy you would expect if apply were meant to read the
      inputs. The fix ORs the two, so those two callers behave exactly as before.

  patches/0012-json-schema-a-schema-no-value-satisfies.patch

      oh-my-pi's `task` tool declares `"model?": "never"` in its batch form, and Arktype exports
      `never` as `{"not": {}}` -- as TypeBox does Type.Never. 0004 refuses `not` outright, so the
      tool-call grammar for every request carrying that tool failed to build and the request with
      it. `not` of the empty schema is not a constraint that has to be complemented: it makes the
      schema unsatisfiable, the same schema as `false`, and that is exactly representable where it
      occurs. An object property of that kind is a key the object never carries -- no rule, and
      its name kept out of an additionalProperties rule -- and a union alternative of that kind is
      dropped. The tagged-argument builders (chat-auto-parser-generator.cpp, and chat.cpp's
      `string="true|false"` format), which give every argument an arm of its own and so never see
      the object, give an optional one of that kind no arm; a model that writes it reaches the
      undeclared-argument arm of 0010 where the format has one. core/text/chatfmt.cpp, the
      declared-format grammar, does the same outside this tree. Where a value is required (a
      required property, the root, an array's items, a union with no alternative left) it is still
      refused, by name. Every other `not` is refused as 0004 refuses it.

  patches/0013-json-schema-a-count-past-int-is-no-bound.patch

      minLength, maxLength, minItems and maxItems were read with get<int>(), which casts: a bound
      past INT_MAX wrapped, and Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER -- a common way for a schema generator to
      write "no limit" -- came out as -1 and the grammar failed to parse, failing the request with
      it. A count at or past INT_MAX is now INT_MAX, which build_repetition already reads as no
      maximum. That is exact for anything served here: no context holds a string or an array that
      long. A count that is negative or not a number is refused by name. A float count is
      truncated, where maxItems used to ignore one. The other half of the same report -- any count
      past 2000 refused -- was the GBNF parser's, and is fixed in core/sample/gbnf.cpp.

  patches/0014-json-schema-a-not-of-every-type-is-never.patch

      0012 recognised `never` only as `{"not": {}}`. oh-my-pi 18.6.0 sends its `task` tool's
      optional `model` as the `not` of a union of all six JSON types -- `{"not": {"anyOf":
      [{"type": "string"}, {"type": "number"}, {"type": "boolean"}, {"type": "object"},
      {"type": "array"}, {"type": "null"}]}}` -- so under every template that constrains tool
      arguments the request still failed with "Unsupported schema keyword \"not\"". A `not` is now
      `never` whenever what it negates accepts every value: `{}` or `true`, a type list naming all
      six types, or a union of bare types that between them name all six (an alternative that is
      itself universal settles it). Annotations may sit beside; any other keyword narrows the
      schema, an alternative that constrains more than its type is not counted, and `integer` does
      not stand for `number`, so a `not` is only read as `never` when it is. Every other `not` is
      refused as 0004 refuses it. The comment of 0012 above is_unsatisfiable, which 0013 had left
      above _schema_count, is back beside the function it describes.
